  "normalizedModelResponse": {
    "episodeSummary": "The patient, a 79-year-old female with a history of hypertension and chronic kidney disease, presented with urosepsis. She developed new onset atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response (RVR) at a rate of 150 bpm, coinciding with a fever spike to 38.9°C. Hemodynamically, she is unstable, requiring low-dose norepinephrine, with a MAP of 67 mmHg and systolic BP of 92 mmHg. Labs show elevated WBC (18.4), lactate (3.1 mmol/L), creatinine (1.7 mg/dL), and procalcitonin (6.2 ng/mL). Electrolytes (potassium, magnesium) are within normal limits. ECG shows narrow QRS complexes, absent P waves, and rate-related ST depression.",
    "rhythmInterpretation": "Authoritative upstream diagnosis: Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response. Source: SLM_Eval. The rhythm diagnosis was not generated by the SLM.",
    "clinicalContext": "Unstable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response (RVR) in the context of sepsis.",
    "mostLikelyEtiology": "Sepsis-induced atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response. The patient has clinical and laboratory evidence of sepsis (fever, elevated WBC, elevated lactate, elevated procalcitonin) and developed new onset atrial fibrillation with RVR during this acute illness.",
